{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, jointly increase batch size from 128 to 256 and learning rate from 6e-4 to 8e-4. Larger batches may reduce gradient noise, permitting moderately faster updates while retaining width 48 and dropout 0.10. Keep features, embedding tying, six causal blocks, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer type fixed. Train one independent model per category with seed 42, at most 200 epochs and exactly 200 batches per epoch.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, change only the existing training-frequency feature exponent from 0.50 to 0.40: feature=(log1p(training_count)/max_log_count)**0.40. Since exponent 0.60 regressed, test stronger attenuation of middle-frequency history items while retaining the existing 1-0.3*feature formula. Compute counts solely from training interactions, keep unseen-item features zero, and preserve embeddings, topology, readout, loss and optimizer. Retain one checkpoint per category and the existing feature buffer.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, change each NextItNet block's residual update from residual+y to residual+0.5*y. Scaling convolutional updates may stabilize the six-block stack and preserve useful input information without removing causal convolutions. Keep block count, dilation schedule, features, embeddings, sequence readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Train one model per category; ensure the predictor writer loads the same residual-scaled architecture and records compatible metadata.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, increase the padding-aware uniform recent-four-state mean contribution from 0.20 to 0.30: output=0.70*current_state+0.30*recent_mean. The regression at 0.10 motivates testing stronger local-interest smoothing while preserving the successful four-state window and uniform weighting. Keep features, embedding parameterization, causal backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Preserve causal training and final-real-position inference; update readout metadata and the predictor writer consistently, with one checkpoint per category."
}
